<!-- WHY VISIT -->
<section class="section"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Why Visit Dunhuang</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="why-grid">
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#128510;</div><h3>Mogao Grottoes</h3><p>Stand before one of the world's greatest repositories of Buddhist art — 492 caves containing 45,000 square meters of murals and over 2,000 painted sculptures spanning 1,000 years of artistic creation.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127965;</div><h3>Desert Oasis</h3><p>Witness the surreal sight of Crescent Moon Spring — a crystal-clear lake shaped like a crescent moon nestled among towering 250-meter sand dunes that have never buried it in 2,000 years.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">🗺️</div><h3>Silk Road Frontier</h3><p>Dunhuang was the last stop in China proper before caravans ventured into the Taklamakan Desert. Visit Yumen Pass, where ancient traders said farewell to Chinese civilization before their perilous westward journey.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127754;</div><h3>Otherworldly Landscapes</h3><p>Explore the Yardang National Geological Park, a vast wind-eroded landscape of bizarre rock formations known as the "Ghost City" — one of China's most dramatic and photogenic desert landscapes.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- TOP ATTRACTIONS -->
<section class="section section-alt"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Top Attractions</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="tours-grid">
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Mogao Caves" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#1</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Mogao Caves (Thousand Buddha Grottoes)</h3><p>A UNESCO World Heritage site and the crowning jewel of Silk Road art. Carved into cliffs along the Daquan River, the caves preserve Buddhist murals and sculptures from the 4th to 14th centuries. The 45-minute guided visit rotates through different caves to protect the fragile art — every visit reveals something new.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Singing Sand Dunes" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#2</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Singing Sand Dunes (Mingsha Shan)</h3><p>These magnificent dunes, rising 250 meters above the desert floor, produce a mysterious humming sound when the wind blows across them — hence the name "Singing Sands." Ride a camel caravan at sunset, try sandboarding, or simply climb to the ridge for a breathtaking panorama of the desert meeting the oasis.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Crescent Moon Spring" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#3</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Crescent Moon Spring (Yueyaquan)</h3><p>This natural wonder — a crescent-shaped lake of clear water surrounded by towering dunes — has existed for 2,000 years. Traditional pavilions and a pagoda beside the water create an almost mirage-like scene. Visit at sunrise or sunset for the most magical photographs when the light paints the dunes gold.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Yumen Pass" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#4</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Yumen Pass (Jade Gate)</h3><p>The legendary frontier pass where Silk Road caravans exited the Chinese empire and entered the vast, dangerous Taklamakan Desert. Today, a weathered beacon tower and crumbling Han Dynasty walls are all that remain — but standing here, you can almost hear the camel bells of ancient traders echoing across the Gobi.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/central-asia-architecture.jpg" alt="Yardang Ghost City" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#5</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Yardang Ghost City</h3><p>About 180km from Dunhuang lies one of China's most surreal landscapes — a vast expanse of wind-carved rock formations resembling ancient castles, ships, and mythical beasts. The eerie howling of wind through these "ghost" formations at dusk gives the park its name. A must for photographers and geology enthusiasts.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- BEST TIME TO VISIT -->
<section class="section"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Best Time to Visit</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="why-grid">
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127800;</div><h3>Spring (Apr-May)</h3><p>Mild (10-25&deg;C) with clear skies. Ideal for exploring Yardang Ghost City and Yumen Pass before the summer heat arrives. Wildflowers occasionally bloom after rare spring rains — a spectacular contrast against the desert.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127774;</div><h3>Autumn (Sep-Oct)</h3><p>THE best season — perfect temperatures (10-25&deg;C), crystal-clear air, and golden light that makes the dunes glow. September is ideal: the summer crowds have gone but the weather remains perfect. Book Mogao Caves tickets well in advance.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#9924;</div><h3>Summer (Jun-Aug)</h3><p>Extremely hot (30-40&deg;C) with intense sun. Visit outdoor sites early morning or late afternoon. The Singing Sand Dunes are especially dramatic at sunset. Carry plenty of water and sun protection. Mogao Caves are air-conditioned.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#10052;</div><h3>Winter (Nov-Mar)</h3><p>Very cold (-10 to 5&deg;C) with possible snow on the dunes — a rare and beautiful sight. Minimal crowds mean you might have the Mogao Caves nearly to yourself. Lower hotel rates. The dunes under snow create once-in-a-lifetime photos.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

